Source: homelab-roundcube-invite
Section: web
Priority: optional
Maintainer: Jeff Gardner <mailmasker-api01@forge.name>
Build-Depends: debhelper-compat (= 13)
Standards-Version: 4.6.2
Homepage: https://github.com/permittivity2/homelab

Package: homelab-roundcube-invite
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends}, homelab-roundcube (>= 0.1.17)
Description: "Send Invite" add-on for an already-installed homelab-roundcube
 A pure PHP Roundcube plugin (invite_sender), shipped as its own
 package rather than bundled into homelab-roundcube itself -- this is
 nothing without Roundcube already installed and configured (hence the
 hard Depends, not a Recommends), so it's an add-on in the true sense:
 postinst verifies homelab-roundcube's own config.inc.php.bootstrapped
 marker exists before touching anything, refusing gracefully (not
 failing the whole apt transaction) if Roundcube isn't set up yet.
 .
 Adds a "Send Invite" Settings tab, calling homelab-invite's own
 /api/v1/invites/* through homelab-api's gateway with the SAME JWT
 Roundcube's own OAuth core already holds for this session's IMAP/SMTP
 (same trust boundary as the existing recipient_blocking plugin, which
 this package's own code is directly modeled on). Holds NO database
 credential of its own -- see homelab-invite/README.md for why this
 split was chosen over giving the plugin its own Postgres role.
 .
 Sends the actual invite email itself, using Roundcube's own native,
 already-authenticated mail delivery (real DKIM alignment to the
 sender's own domain, zero new mail-sending code) -- homelab-invite
 only mints the token/link for this channel, unlike CLI-initiated
 invites where homelab-invite sends the email itself.
 .
 Because Roundcube writes config.inc.php exactly once (a
 .bootstrapped marker, never rewritten by dpkg-reconfigure), this
 package's own postinst makes a structurally-anchored, idempotent edit
 to the ALREADY-WRITTEN $config['plugins'] array to register itself --
 the first package in this ecosystem to mutate a sibling package's live
 config rather than only ever writing its own. See README.md for the
 exact technique and its safety net (backs the file up first, validates
 with php -l, restores on failure).
